<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Jin</h1><p class="st-meta">266 CE – 316 CE · capital Luoyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sima family's dynasty that reunited China in 280 after the Three Kingdoms, only to tear itself apart in the War of the Eight Princes and lose the north.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After decades in which the Sima family dominated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin">Sima Yan</a> forced the last Wei emperor to abdicate in 266 and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a> was already conquered, and in 280 Jin forces took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-wu">Eastern Wu</a>, reuniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> gave imperial princes their own armies and fiefs, demobilised provincial forces and introduced land allotment rules. The court and great families were famous for extravagance, and the Pure Conversation pioneered under Wei by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-sages-of-the-bamboo-grove">Seven Sages of the Bamboo Grove</a> flourished. Under Emperor Hui, who was mentally incapable, Empress Jia's intrigues triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-eight-princes">War of the Eight Princes</a> from 291 to 306.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Exhausted by civil war, the dynasty could not resist risings by peoples settled inside the frontier. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a> armies sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 311 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 316, capturing Emperor Min. A branch of the Sima family continued the dynasty in the south as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-jin.jpg" alt="Western Jin" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Jeun_Dynasty_280_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>266 CE – 316 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luoyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emperor Wu of Jin, Emperor Hui of Jin, Emperor Min of Jin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion, Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jin dynasty (266-420), Western Jin dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=291&amp;focus=western-jin" aria-label="Western Jin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7535 923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Jin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7855 1179L7855 1179M7855 1179L8057 1267M7855 1179L7810 1131M7855 1179L7855 1179M7855 1179L7743 987M7855 1179L7669 1147"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M8057 1267h0M7810 1131h0M7855 1179h0M7743 987h0M7669 1147h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Luoyang. <a href="/atlas?year=291&amp;focus=western-jin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
